// Original work, written for STORYMACHINE_STRUCTURAL_FORM_EXPERIMENT_2026-08-03.
// Kishotenketsu arm, Pair 1 (n=12 scenes). Follows server/lib/structure-presets.ts
// kishotenketsu beat template exactly: Ki 0-25% (scenes 1-3, no antagonism),
// Sho 25-50% (scenes 4-6, discovery not confrontation), Ten 50-75% (scenes 7-9,
// twist from outside the conflict axis), Ketsu 75-100% (scenes 10-12, synthesis,
// no winners/losers). Scenes 1-2 are textually identical to the matched
// three-act control (pair1-three-act-control.fountain) — both templates call
// for pure, conflict-free establishment at that point, so craft is held
// constant exactly where the beat maps agree, and diverges only where they
// diverge (scene 3 onward).

INT. CAPE HALLOW LIGHTHOUSE - SPIRAL STAIR - DAWN

MIRA VOSS, 71, spine straight as the rail, climbs with a rag and a tin of brass polish tucked under one arm. Gulls needle the wind below. The great lens throws pink dawn light across the curved walls in slow, deliberate sweeps.

MIRA
(to the lens, fondly)
Still slower than you used to be. Aren't we both.

She reaches the gallery, sets the tin down, begins working salt crust from the brass fittings.

EXT. LIGHTHOUSE DOCK - LATER

A small ferry idles at the pier. PEARL VOSS, 24, hair wind-whipped, hauls a duffel bag off the gangplank. Mira waits at the end of the dock, wiping polish from her hands with a rag.

PEARL
You said you'd stopped climbing the tower yourself.

MIRA
I said I'd stopped enjoying it. Different thing entirely.

Pearl laughs, drops the bag, hugs her grandmother hard enough to rock her back on her heels.

INT. LIGHTHOUSE KITCHEN - EVENING

Mira ladles chowder into two bowls at a scarred wooden table. A row of logbooks, spines cracked, lines the windowsill behind her.

PEARL
Do you still write in these? The logs?

MIRA
Every night the light's lit. Forty-one years of weather and nothing else worth reporting.

PEARL
(flipping one open)
Grandpa's handwriting's all through the early ones.

MIRA
He never trusted me to get the wind speed right. Kept correcting my sevens.

INT. LIGHTHOUSE WORKSHOP - DAY

Sunlight through a salt-fogged window. Pearl roots through a drawer of tools and finds a thick envelope, corners softened with handling, unopened, addressed to Mira from the Coastal Heritage Authority. Postmarked eight months ago.

PEARL
This has been sitting under a socket wrench for eight months.

MIRA
(taking it, setting it on a shelf without looking at it)
Then it can sit a while longer.

PEARL
You don't want to know what it says?

MIRA
I know what these letters say. I've gotten three of them since your grandfather died.

EXT. LIGHTHOUSE GALLERY - SUNSET

Mira guides Pearl's hand along the lens housing, showing her the rotation gears.

MIRA
Four turns a minute, always. Your grandfather timed it against his own pulse once — said if the gears ever matched his heartbeat exactly, he'd know something was wrong.

PEARL
Did they ever match?

MIRA
Once. The night you were born.

A beat between them. Pearl looks at the mechanism with new tenderness.

INT. LIGHTHOUSE WORKSHOP - NIGHT

Mira alone. The envelope sits on the workbench in lamplight. She turns it over twice without opening it, sets a coffee mug on top of it like a paperweight, and switches off the lamp.

INT. LIGHTHOUSE KITCHEN - NIGHT

Wind slams the shutters. The power flickers and dies. Pearl lights a hurricane lamp; on the table between them, the envelope, unmoved from where it's sat all summer. Mira finally slides a thumbnail under the flap.

PEARL
You don't have to—

MIRA
If it's going to end something, I'd rather it end it by lamplight, in my own kitchen, with you here.

She unfolds three pages. Reads. Her face changes — not falling, opening.

MIRA (CONT'D)
It's not what I thought it was.

EXT. LIGHTHOUSE GALLERY - NIGHT (STORM CONTINUES)

Mira and Pearl huddle under the gallery overhang, the beam still turning behind them on battery backup. Mira holds the pages against her chest, out of the rain.

MIRA
It's not a decommission notice. It's a trust. Your grandfather set it up eleven years ago — before the cancer, before any of it. Funded the whole preservation status out of his own patents. Named a keeper of record.

PEARL
Who?

MIRA
Me. And after me — "kin of Mira's choosing." In his handwriting. Filled in blank, waiting.

PEARL
Waiting for what?

MIRA
(quiet, looking at her)
I think he already knew.

INT. LIGHTHOUSE WORKSHOP - NIGHT

Pearl runs her fingers along the brass gears of the mechanism Tomas built, seeing it differently now. Scratched faintly into the underside of the housing, in tiny letters: P.V., WHEN YOU'RE READY. She goes very still.

EXT. LIGHTHOUSE LAMP ROOM - DAWN

Storm cleared. The backup battery dies with a soft click. Mira and Pearl turn the manual crank together, hand over hand, until the main gear catches and the lamp resumes its own rotation.

MIRA
There. Back to itself.

PEARL
Or forward to itself.

Mira almost smiles at that.

EXT. LIGHTHOUSE DOCK - MORNING

A COASTAL AUTHORITY OFFICER, unhurried, folder under her arm, meets them at the dock — not to shut anything down, but with transfer paperwork for the trust.

OFFICER
Just needs a second signature. Ms. Voss, if you're accepting keeper status.

Pearl looks to Mira, who nods once. Pearl signs.

EXT. LIGHTHOUSE GALLERY - DUSK

Mira and Pearl watch the beam sweep the darkening water side by side. Mira holds out the tin of brass polish and the rag.

MIRA
Sevens. Try not to get them wrong.

PEARL
(taking it)
No promises.

They stand together as the light turns, unhurried, the last light completing another slow revolution over them both.
